[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Concurrency, Threads & ExecutorService in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building responsive Android applications requires careful management of background tasks. Performing heavy operations on the main thread (UI thread) will cause your application to freeze, leading to the dreaded Application Not Responding (ANR) dialog. In this tutorial, we will explore how to handle concurrency safely using raw threads and modern ExecutorService utilities in Java."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"The Problem with the Main Thread"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Android UI thread is responsible for drawing views, handling touch events, and updating user interfaces. If you block this thread with network requests, database operations, or complex calculations, the system cannot render frames smoothly. Consequently, users experience stuttering or complete freezes."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Raw Java Threads"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most fundamental way to run code off the main thread is by creating and starting a new Thread. While simple, managing raw threads manually can become inefficient and error-prone in large applications."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"Thread backgroundThread = new Thread(new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // Perform heavy operations here
        String result = "Data downloaded";

        // Update UI if needed via Handler
        runOnUiThread(new Runnable() {
